<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sar-e Pol-e Zahab</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sar-e Pol-e Zahab is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the ancient route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. Rock reliefs in the gorge nearby include the victory relief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullubi">Lullubi</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anubanini">Anubanini</a> from the late third millennium BCE. The town was badly damaged by an earthquake in 2017.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sar-e-pol-e-zahab.jpg" alt="Sar-e Pol-e Zahab" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: کامبیز · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B7%D8%A7%D9%82_%DA%AF%D8%B1%D8%A7-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Sar-e Pol-e Zahab</b> a Lullubi king, Anubanini, carved his own relief with the goddess Inanna, imitating Akkadian royal art.</blockquote><a href="/state/lullubi">Lullubi</a></li></ul></section>
